## Context

New main-text figure for section 4: a label-free *source-reliability* screen
plotted against reference-based accuracy, showing that the inaccurate models are
also the unreliable ones and can be screened out without ground truth.

Pipeline: read `experiments/derived/exp1_cross_eval.csv` (parametric arm) ->
`src/aedist/plot_exp1_reliability.py` -> PDF + macros, wired in
`experiments/render.mk`. Figures-are-artifacts rule applies (no inline plots).

**Blocked-by 0505:** the y-axis is run-grain reliability; it is wrong until the
uniform-as-of scoring fix lands. Do not build on the current CSV.

## Definitions (ratified across the 2026-06-09 Imagine session)

- **good run** = a run with no zero on any of the 12 reference-free dimensions
  (coherence x3 incl. 1-veto, provenance x4, temporality x3, completeness x2).
  Missing score = not a doubt (skip; only `high_conf_dual_source` is ever
  missing, 4/70). One doubt taints the whole run — the gate is a minimum, not an
  average (no laundering).
- **Reliability** (y) = count of good runs out of 5 reps. Report the **count
  0..5**, not a percentage. It is the expected number of good runs.
- **Accuracy** (x) = **average** (not median) F1 over the good runs — the
  expectation of one good run. Median was rejected: it presupposes multiple runs,
  and multi-run usage should be fusion (tracker 0465), not middle-picking. For a
  model with zero good runs, x = average F1 over all runs (no good run exists;
  caption must say so).
- Markers sit exactly on (F1, integer count) — data integrity is paramount,
  no jitter except <= one dot-radius nudge on exact collisions (none occur on
  the F1 axis). Labels centred above, flipped below to de-collide, short leader.

## Title / labels (author-set)

- Title: "Inaccurate models (low F1) are also unreliable (few good runs). They
  can be screened out."
- Subtitle: "good run = no zero score on 12 dimensions"
- Y: "Reliability (good runs out of 5 reps)", ticks 0..5.
- X: "Accuracy (average F1 over good runs)".

